@flexiarg creativity-commons/affected-participants-change-the-rules
@title Affected Participants Change The Rules
@keywords collective choice, operational rules, tests, Conway's law, Ostrom 3
@audience institution designers, operators, agents
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Ostrom design principle 3 (collective-choice arrangements), as transposed in Corneli 2016 (ICCC 2016), Table 2 and section "3. Collective-choice arrangements" (Ostrom 2009; Ostrom et al. 2012 p. 79; Conway 1968). Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29. Derived from the paper, not mined from runs.
@transposes institutions/collective-choice-arrangements
@status draft 2026-10-29; runs correct it
@references [creativity-commons/ARGUMENT institutions/rules-as-institutional-statements creativity-commons/producers-can-modify-the-tests]

! conclusion:
  Most Producers who are affected by the rules governing contribution
  can participate in modifying the operational rules; since each rule
  contains a logical test, changing the rules means writing new tests.

  + context:
    Participation is lossy in practice: changes are made by those most
    affected, and therefore most knowledgeable. By Conway's law the rules
    they write will mirror the structure of the group that wrote them.

  + IF:
    Rules constrain what Producers may do, and the Producers meet cases
    the rules handle badly.

  + HOWEVER:
    Open rule-changing invites churn and self-serving edits; closed
    rule-changing leaves the people who see the defects unable to fix
    them.

  + THEN:
    Give affected Producers a recorded path to propose a rule change,
    written as a new or changed test, with the run or case that motivated
    it. Expect the result to reflect the proposers' situation, and check
    it against the cases of those who did not take part.

  + BECAUSE:
    The people who meet a rule's failures hold the information needed to
    fix it. Writing the change as a test makes it checkable, and citing
    the motivating case keeps the change tied to practice rather than to
    preference.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Example: agents' review findings and incidents become CLAUDE.md rules with the dated incident cited; see patterns-corrected-by-practice.]
